// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["xvii"] = {"qs": [["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "A self-employed doctor has no employer pension. Compared with a government employee covered by an old defined-benefit pension, her main additional need is to:", ["Build her own retirement corpus through voluntary saving", "Avoid all equity investments to preserve capital", "Rely on gratuity under the Payment of Gratuity Act", "Depend on the Employees' Pension Scheme"], 0, "Self-employed people have no employer pension, gratuity or EPS, so they must create their own corpus through products like NPS, PPF and mutual funds. Avoiding equity entirely would raise inflation risk."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "A client's target allocation is 40% equity and 60% debt. After a strong year, equity has risen to 52% of the portfolio. Rebalancing means:", ["Selling some equity and buying debt to restore 40:60", "Buying more equity to ride the momentum", "Switching entirely to debt to lock in gains", "Doing nothing until equity falls back"], 0, "Rebalancing returns the portfolio to its target risk level by selling the asset that has grown above its weight and buying the one below. It is a discipline, not a market call."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "A subscriber wants the highest possible annuity income now but also wants his wife to continue receiving pension after his death. Which option balances these needs?", ["Joint-life annuity without return of purchase price", "Single-life annuity with return of purchase price", "Single-life annuity without return of purchase price", "Annuity certain for five years only"], 0, "A joint-life annuity continues to the spouse; choosing it without return of purchase price keeps the payout higher than a return-of-purchase-price variant. A single-life annuity stops at his death."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Annuity service providers that pay pensions to NPS subscribers on exit are:", ["Life insurers empanelled by PFRDA", "Pension fund managers of NPS", "Scheduled commercial banks acting as POPs", "Mutual funds offering monthly income schemes"], 0, "Only IRDAI-registered life insurers empanelled by PFRDA can act as ASPs for NPS annuities. Pension fund managers manage accumulation, not annuity payment."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "A retiree has a corpus of ₹1.2 crore and withdraws ₹4.8 lakh in the first year. His initial withdrawal rate is:", ["2.5%", "4%", "5%", "4.8%"], 1, "Withdrawal rate = 4.8 lakh ÷ 120 lakh = 4%. 4.8% would treat ₹4.8 lakh as a percentage directly."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A client wants ₹1 crore in 20 years and expects 10% a year compounded monthly. How much must he invest at the end of each month (approximately)?", ["₹41,667", "₹13,169", "₹9,650", "₹16,700"], 1, "Using the future value of an annuity formula with r = 10%/12 and n = 240, the monthly SIP ≈ ₹13,169. ₹41,667 simply divides ₹1 crore by 240, ignoring returns."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Uncommuted (monthly) pension received by a retired employee from a former employer is taxed as:", ["Income from other sources", "Capital gains", "Salary income", "Exempt income"], 2, "Pension received from a former employer is treated as salary, so the standard deduction applies. Pension received by a family member after the employee's death is treated differently."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A client has ₹25 lakh in retirement savings that will grow at 7% a year for 10 years until he retires. What will this amount to at retirement (approximately)?", ["₹42.5 lakh", "₹35.0 lakh", "₹49.2 lakh", "₹57.6 lakh"], 2, "FV = 25 × 1.07^10 ≈ 25 × 1.967 ≈ ₹49.2 lakh. ₹42.5 lakh is simple interest (25 + 25 × 7% × 10)."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "Under a will, a person's mutual fund units are left to his daughter, but his son is the registered nominee. Generally, the nominee:", ["Becomes the absolute owner, overriding the will", "Cannot receive the units until the will is challenged", "Receives the units from the fund but holds them for the legal heirs as per the will", "Must sell the units and pay the proceeds to the government"], 2, "In most financial assets, the nominee is a custodian who receives the asset for the benefit of the legal heirs or beneficiaries under the will. Nomination does not override succession."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "Which statement about the impact of medical inflation on retirement planning is most accurate?", ["Medical costs fall after retirement because people work less", "Government schemes fully cover all private hospital expenses of retirees", "Healthcare costs often rise faster than general inflation, so they need a separate provision", "Health costs can be ignored if the retiree has a large equity portfolio"], 2, "Medical inflation in India typically exceeds headline inflation, and health needs rise with age. Planners therefore provide for health insurance and a separate medical reserve rather than assuming general inflation."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "For a non-government employee who also receives gratuity, how much of the commuted pension is exempt from tax?", ["One-third of the full value of the pension commuted", "One-half of the commuted amount", "The entire commuted amount", "None of it"], 0, "For non-government employees receiving gratuity, one-third of the commuted value of full pension is exempt; without gratuity, one-half is exempt. Government employees get full exemption."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Compared with the aggressive life-cycle fund, the conservative life-cycle fund under Auto Choice:", ["Keeps equity constant throughout the subscriber's life", "Invests only in corporate bonds", "Is available only to Government sector subscribers", "Starts with a lower maximum equity allocation"], 3, "The conservative life-cycle fund starts with a lower equity cap than the moderate and aggressive funds, and all reduce equity with age. It is available to non-government subscribers choosing Auto Choice."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Why does a life-cycle fund reduce equity exposure as the subscriber approaches retirement?", ["Because equities always underperform debt over long periods", "Because PFRDA does not permit equity after the age of 45", "To lower the risk of a large loss just before the corpus is needed", "To maximise returns in the final years of saving when the corpus is at its largest"], 2, "As retirement nears, there is less time to recover from a market fall, so de-risking protects the corpus. Equities usually outperform over long periods, which is why they dominate early on."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "A private-sector employee withdraws his EPF balance after three years of continuous service to start a business. The withdrawal is generally:", ["Fully exempt in all cases", "Taxable, because continuous service is less than five years", "Exempt only if he invests in NPS", "Taxable only on the employer's contribution and never on interest"], 1, "EPF withdrawals before five years of continuous service are generally taxable unless termination is due to reasons beyond the employee's control. After five years, withdrawals are exempt."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "Which of the following is the best description of the 'accumulation phase' in retirement planning?", ["The years after retirement when the corpus is drawn down", "The working years in which savings are built up for retirement", "The period in which an annuity is paying out to the retiree", "The time taken to settle the retiree's estate after death"], 1, "The accumulation phase covers working years when contributions are made and invested. The distribution or decumulation phase comes after retirement, when savings are drawn down."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "A retiree suffers heavy equity losses in the first two years of retirement while also withdrawing money. Even if markets recover later, his corpus may not. This illustrates:", ["Interest-rate risk on deposits", "Currency risk", "Sequence-of-returns risk", "Concentration risk in a single stock"], 2, "Sequence-of-returns risk arises because withdrawals during early losses lock in those losses, leaving a smaller base to benefit from later recovery. The order of returns matters during withdrawal."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "Why might a retiree with a disabled child consider setting up a private trust?", ["To avoid all income tax permanently on the family's assets and on the child's income", "Because wills are not valid for parents of disabled children", "To manage assets for the child's lifetime through a trustee after the parents' death", "To transfer assets without any documentation"], 2, "A trust lets a trustee manage and use assets for the child's benefit over the long term. It does not remove all taxes, and wills remain valid; a trust simply provides ongoing management."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "A retiree has a base health policy of ₹5 lakh. A super top-up policy will:", ["Replace the base policy entirely", "Pay a fixed daily cash amount regardless of bills", "Pay hospital bills above a deductible once the aggregate claims in a year exceed it", "Cover only outpatient consultations"], 2, "A super top-up pays when total claims in a policy year cross the deductible, making it a cost-effective way to raise cover above the base policy. Hospital cash plans pay fixed daily amounts."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "Which of the following can a PPF subscriber do after the account completes its initial tenure?", ["Convert it into an NPS Tier I account", "Transfer it to the employer for EPF credit", "Withdraw only the interest and leave the principal locked forever", "Extend it in blocks, with or without further contributions"], 3, "On maturity, a PPF account can be extended in blocks with or without fresh contributions, or closed. It cannot be converted to NPS or EPF."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Which of the following is a power of PFRDA under the PFRDA Act, 2013?", ["Fixing interest rates on PPF and small savings schemes", "Deciding EPF interest rates each year", "Granting licences to life insurers to sell annuities", "Registering and regulating intermediaries and protecting subscriber interests"], 3, "PFRDA registers and regulates NPS intermediaries and protects subscribers. Small savings rates are set by the government, EPF rates are decided under the EPF framework, and insurers are licensed by IRDAI."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "In NPS, which intermediary is responsible for the safekeeping of securities bought by pension fund managers?", ["The trustee bank", "The custodian", "The Point of Presence", "The retirement adviser"], 1, "The custodian holds and settles the securities in which NPS money is invested. The trustee bank handles fund flows, not securities."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "A retired couple wants to ensure that if one becomes mentally incapacitated, the other can manage bank accounts and investments. A useful legal tool is:", ["A durable power of attorney", "A gift deed", "A nomination form", "A rent agreement"], 0, "A power of attorney authorises another person to act on one's behalf. A nomination works only after death, while a gift deed transfers ownership outright."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "A retiree buying a new health policy at 62 should pay special attention to:", ["The insurer's share price", "Whether premiums are deductible under the new tax regime", "Waiting periods for pre-existing diseases and co-payment clauses", "The policy's maturity benefit"], 2, "Senior citizen policies often have waiting periods, co-payment and sub-limits that affect claims. Health policies do not pay maturity benefits, and premium deduction is an old-regime benefit."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "A retiree's biggest worry is that she may live to 95 and exhaust her savings. This is known as:", ["Liquidity risk", "Reinvestment risk", "Longevity risk", "Credit risk of the issuer of her bonds"], 2, "Longevity risk is the risk of outliving one's savings because life turns out longer than planned. Liquidity, reinvestment and credit risks are different investment risks."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "A reverse mortgage is most suitable for which retiree?", ["A tenant looking for a cheaper rental", "A homeowner with low income who wishes to stay in the house", "A retiree wanting to buy a second home", "A young professional saving for a down payment"], 1, "A reverse mortgage lets senior homeowners convert home equity into income while continuing to live in the house. It needs a self-owned residential property."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A client needs ₹2 crore in 15 years. What amount must be saved at the end of each year if investments earn 10% a year (approximately)?", ["₹13.33 lakh", "₹4.80 lakh", "₹8.10 lakh", "₹6.29 lakh"], 3, "Annual saving = 2 crore × 0.10 ÷ (1.10^15 − 1) ≈ ₹6.29 lakh. ₹13.33 lakh divides the target by 15 years and ignores compounding."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "Why is the real rate of return used when computing the corpus for an inflation-indexed retirement income?", ["It allows payments in today's terms to be discounted while allowing for rising expenses", "It always gives a higher corpus than nominal calculations do", "It removes the need to estimate life expectancy, since real returns adjust for longevity", "Because inflation does not affect expenses after retirement"], 0, "Using the real rate converts a growing stream into constant real payments, simplifying the calculation of a corpus that must fund inflation-linked withdrawals. Life expectancy is still needed."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "Which online system lets an NPS subscriber lodge and track grievances?", ["SEBI SCORES", "IRDAI Bima Bharosa", "The Central Grievance Management System of the CRAs", "RBI Integrated Ombudsman portal for complaints against banks acting as POPs"], 2, "NPS grievances are registered and tracked through CGMS hosted by the CRAs. SCORES, Bima Bharosa and RBI's portal cover securities, insurance and banking complaints respectively."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "A subscriber may defer buying the annuity at exit. The main advantage of deferment is that:", ["The corpus becomes exempt from all market risk during the whole period of deferment", "The corpus remains invested and the annuity can be bought later, possibly at better rates", "It allows him to withdraw 100% in cash later", "Annuity rates are guaranteed to rise each year of deferment"], 1, "Deferring the annuity keeps money invested and lets the subscriber choose a later time, perhaps when he needs income or rates are better. The corpus still carries market risk and rates are not guaranteed."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "Why does a retiree's need for life insurance usually decline after retirement?", ["Dependants are fewer and liabilities such as home loans are usually paid off", "Life insurance is not available after 60", "Pension income is fully exempt from tax", "Health insurance replaces life insurance automatically once a person turns sixty"], 0, "Life insurance replaces income for dependants. In retirement, children are typically independent and loans repaid, reducing the need. Some cover may still be useful for a spouse or estate purposes."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Which pension arrangement is designed for workers in the unorganised sector and guarantees a fixed minimum pension backed by the government?", ["Employees' Pension Scheme", "NPS corporate model", "Voluntary Provident Fund", "Atal Pension Yojana"], 3, "APY targets unorganised-sector workers and offers a government-backed fixed pension based on contribution level. EPS covers organised-sector employees, and VPF is an EPF add-on."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "A retiree earns 8% on his investments while inflation is 6%. His real rate of return (exact) is:", ["1.89%", "2.00%", "14.48%", "1.33%"], 0, "Real return = (1.08 ÷ 1.06) − 1 ≈ 1.89%. Simple subtraction gives 2%, which is only an approximation."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "An employee voluntarily contributes more than the mandatory 12% to EPF. This additional contribution is called:", ["Employees' Deposit Linked Insurance", "Employer's matching contribution", "Pension Fund Tier II", "Voluntary Provident Fund"], 3, "Contributions above the mandatory rate go to the Voluntary Provident Fund, which earns the EPF rate. EDLI is a life insurance scheme, and the employer is not required to match VPF."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "A retiree's portfolio includes 70% in a single private-sector company's shares, where he worked for 30 years. The main risk the adviser should highlight is:", ["Inflation risk on fixed deposits", "Concentration risk", "Currency risk", "Reinvestment risk on annuities"], 1, "Holding most wealth in one stock exposes the client to company-specific losses. Diversification across issuers and asset classes reduces this risk."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser recommends a product mainly because it pays her a higher commission, though a cheaper suitable product exists. This violates the principle of:", ["Acting in the client's best interest", "Maintaining confidentiality", "Record keeping", "Continuing professional education"], 0, "Advisers must put the client's interest first. Choosing a product for higher commission when a more suitable option exists breaches this duty, even if the product is not wholly unsuitable."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "A key disadvantage of a life annuity without return of purchase price is that:", ["Its payout is lower than all other annuity options", "It stops paying after a fixed term even if the annuitant is alive", "Its payouts change with stock market levels", "Nothing is left for heirs if the annuitant dies early"], 3, "This option gives the highest payout, but on death the purchase price is not returned, so heirs receive nothing. Payments continue for life and do not depend on markets."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "Which document is essential for the nominee to claim NPS benefits after the subscriber's death?", ["The subscriber's latest income-tax return only", "The nominee's EPF passbook", "A no-objection certificate from the subscriber's employer in all cases", "The subscriber's death certificate along with the claim form and KYC"], 3, "Death claims require a death certificate, the withdrawal form and the nominee's KYC and bank details. Income-tax returns and EPF passbooks are not required."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "Why must a retirement adviser keep records of advice given and the client's risk profile?", ["To share with other clients as case studies", "To demonstrate that recommendations were suitable if questioned later", "Because records allow the adviser to justify charging higher fees to long-standing clients", "Records are optional if the client trusts the adviser"], 1, "Records provide evidence of the basis for advice and are required by regulation. They must be kept confidential and are not optional."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "Annuity laddering means:", ["Buying an annuity whose payout increases each year", "Investing the monthly annuity income in equity funds to build a second corpus", "Buying several annuities at different times instead of one at retirement", "Switching annuity providers every year"], 2, "Laddering spreads annuity purchases over time, reducing the risk of locking the whole corpus at low rates and allowing higher rates at older ages."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Which statement about the NPS Tier II account is correct?", ["It is a voluntary account with flexible withdrawals and no lock-in", "It has the same withdrawal restrictions as Tier I", "It can be opened without holding a Tier I account", "Its corpus must be used to buy an annuity at 60"], 0, "Tier II is a voluntary savings facility with free withdrawals. It requires an active Tier I account and has no annuity requirement. Tier I is the restricted pension account."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Interest earned on a Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme account is:", ["Fully exempt like PPF interest", "Taxable only on maturity", "Taxable as income, though senior citizens may claim specified deductions", "Taxed at a flat 1%"], 2, "SCSS interest is taxable at slab rates and attracts TDS above thresholds. Under the old regime, senior citizens may claim a deduction for interest income within limits, but it is not exempt like PPF."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "Which assumption, if overestimated, will most likely cause a retirement corpus to fall short?", ["The expected rate of return on investments", "The client's life expectancy", "The rate of inflation", "The amount of expenses in retirement"], 0, "An overly optimistic return assumption means too little is saved. Overestimating life expectancy, inflation or expenses would instead make the plan more conservative."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "A bank appointed in the NPS structure to receive contributions from POPs and nodal offices and transfer them to pension funds is the:", ["Trustee bank", "Custodian", "Annuity service provider", "Sponsor bank of the pension fund"], 0, "The trustee bank manages the banking side of NPS: it receives contributions and passes them to pension fund managers and pays out withdrawals as instructed. The custodian holds securities."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "A subscriber is dissatisfied with her pension fund's performance. What can she do?", ["Withdraw her entire Tier I corpus immediately without restriction", "Ask the CRA to manage the money directly", "Transfer the corpus to a mutual fund of her choice", "Switch to another pension fund manager as permitted by PFRDA"], 3, "NPS allows subscribers to change their pension fund manager periodically within PFRDA rules. Tier I money cannot be freely withdrawn or moved into mutual funds, and the CRA does not manage investments."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "A retiree wants protection against inflation in her annuity income. Which annuity feature addresses this?", ["A life annuity with return of purchase price", "An increasing annuity whose payout rises at a fixed rate each year", "An annuity certain for ten years", "A joint-life annuity with 50% to the spouse"], 1, "An increasing annuity raises payments periodically, offsetting part of inflation. The other options address legacy, guaranteed term or spouse protection, not inflation."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Which factor is LEAST relevant when a 28-year-old chooses between Auto and Active Choice?", ["The name of the CRA that issued her PRAN", "Her risk appetite and investment knowledge", "Her willingness to review allocations regularly", "Her time horizon to retirement"], 0, "Choice of investment option should depend on risk appetite, time horizon and ability to manage allocations. The CRA only maintains records and has no bearing on investment outcomes."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Which of the following is a function of the Central Recordkeeping Agency for an NPS subscriber?", ["Selecting stocks for the subscriber's equity scheme", "Issuing the PRAN and maintaining records of contributions and units", "Paying the monthly annuity after exit", "Holding the securities bought by pension funds and settling their trades with exchanges"], 1, "The CRA issues PRANs, maintains records of contributions, switches and units, and provides statements. Stock selection is done by pension funds, annuities by ASPs and custody by the custodian."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "Why do most planners keep some equity exposure even for retirees in their sixties?", ["Equity returns are guaranteed over a five-year period", "Retirement may last decades, and equity helps the corpus keep pace with inflation", "Retirees are exempt from tax on equity gains once they cross the age of sixty", "Debt instruments are not available to senior citizens"], 1, "A retirement of 25 to 30 years needs growth to offset inflation. Equity provides that potential but is not guaranteed, and there is no special tax exemption for retirees on equity gains."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Which individual is NOT eligible to open an NPS account under the all-citizen model?", ["A citizen aged 75 seeking to open a new account", "A citizen aged 19 with a savings bank account", "An NRI who is a citizen of India, subject to applicable rules", "A self-employed citizen aged 45"], 0, "The all-citizen model is open to Indian citizens within the prescribed entry age band, which is far below 75. NRIs who are citizens may join subject to FEMA and NPS rules."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "Strategic asset allocation differs from tactical asset allocation in that strategic allocation:", ["Sets long-term target weights based on goals and risk profile", "Shifts weights frequently to exploit short-term market views", "Is applied only to equity mutual funds", "Ignores the client's time horizon"], 0, "Strategic allocation is the long-term policy mix tied to goals and risk tolerance. Tactical allocation involves short-term deviations from it to exploit market opportunities."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "Before recommending an asset allocation, the adviser must first:", ["Understand the client's goals, finances and risk profile", "Select the products with the best past returns", "Ask the client to sign a waiver of liability", "Fix the client's retirement age at 60 regardless of preference"], 0, "Suitability requires understanding the client's needs, circumstances and risk tolerance before recommending. Past returns alone are not a basis for advice."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Rahul, a salaried employee, wants to open NPS online without visiting any branch. Which channel can he use?", ["Only a post office savings bank counter", "eNPS, using Aadhaar or PAN-based online KYC", "Only through his employer's HR department", "A registered mutual fund distributor's offline form"], 1, "eNPS allows individuals to open and fund NPS accounts online using Aadhaar or PAN-based verification. Post offices and employers are other channels but not the only ones."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A retiree needs ₹6 lakh a year in today's terms for 25 years, withdrawn at the start of each year. If the real rate of return (after inflation) is 1.5%, the corpus needed is about:", ["₹1.50 crore", "₹96 lakh", "₹1.26 crore", "₹1.08 crore"], 2, "PV of an annuity due of ₹6 lakh for 25 years at 1.5% ≈ ₹126.2 lakh, i.e. about ₹1.26 crore. ₹1.50 crore ignores returns entirely (6 × 25)."], ["Health, Insurance & Estate Aspects in Retirement", "Which step most reduces disputes over a retiree's estate among his children?", ["Holding all assets in cash at home", "Avoiding nominations to prevent confusion", "Making a clear, properly witnessed will and keeping it accessible", "Transferring everything to one child informally and asking him to share it fairly"], 2, "A clear and valid will, executed with witnesses and known to the executor, minimises disputes. Informal transfers or missing nominations tend to create conflict and delays."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "A 'bucket strategy' for retirement income typically involves:", ["Investing the entire corpus in a single balanced fund and withdrawing a fixed sum every month", "Holding only gold and real estate after retirement", "Spending the equity portion first and keeping cash for later years", "Keeping near-term needs in safe assets and longer-term money in growth assets"], 3, "The bucket approach holds a few years of expenses in liquid, safe assets and invests money for later years in growth assets, reducing the need to sell equities in a downturn."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "A subscriber at 60 wants to receive the lump-sum portion in instalments rather than at once. NPS permits this through:", ["Conversion of Tier I into a fixed deposit with the trustee bank", "Transferring the lump sum to Tier II only", "Systematic withdrawal of the lump sum over a chosen period", "A loan against the NPS corpus"], 2, "NPS offers systematic lump-sum withdrawal, letting the subscriber draw the lump-sum portion periodically while the balance stays invested. NPS does not provide loans against the corpus."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Which entity in the NPS architecture holds the assets of NPS in trust for the benefit of subscribers?", ["The Central Recordkeeping Agency", "The NPS Trust", "The Point of Presence", "The Annuity Service Provider"], 1, "The NPS Trust, set up by PFRDA, holds NPS assets for the benefit of subscribers and oversees pension funds. CRAs keep records, POPs onboard subscribers and ASPs pay annuities."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser tells a 59-year-old client that an equity fund 'will surely give 15% every year'. This is:", ["Acceptable since equity has historically delivered high returns", "Allowed if the client is a senior citizen", "A misleading assurance of returns that must be avoided", "Permitted if mentioned only verbally"], 2, "Market-linked returns cannot be guaranteed. Promising assured returns is misleading and breaches the code of conduct regardless of the mode of communication."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "Which client is likely to have the highest capacity to take equity risk in retirement savings?", ["A 63-year-old retiree with no pension and modest savings", "A 35-year-old with stable income and 25 years to retirement", "A 58-year-old planning to retire in two years with large EMIs", "A 70-year-old who depends fully on his corpus for expenses"], 1, "Risk capacity depends on time horizon, income stability and dependence on the portfolio. A young earner with a long horizon can absorb volatility far better than retirees who depend on the corpus."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "A client asks the adviser to backdate a document to qualify for a tax benefit. The adviser should:", ["Agree if the client is a long-standing customer", "Agree if the amount involved is small", "Forward the request to the CRA for approval", "Refuse, as falsifying documents is unethical and illegal"], 3, "Falsifying records is fraudulent and breaches the code of conduct and law. No relationship or amount justifies it."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Leave encashment received by a private-sector employee at retirement is:", ["Exempt up to a statutory ceiling, with the excess taxable", "Fully taxable as salary without any exemption, whatever the employee's length of service", "Fully exempt regardless of the amount", "Taxed as long-term capital gains"], 0, "Leave encashment at retirement for non-government employees is exempt up to the statutory limit and computation rules; amounts above that are taxable as salary. Government employees enjoy full exemption."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Under the corporate model of NPS, which of the following is true?", ["Employer contributions can be made in addition to the employee's own contributions", "Only the employer can contribute; employees cannot add anything", "The employer chooses the pension fund and employees can never change it", "Employees lose their PRAN on leaving the company"], 0, "In the corporate model, both the employer and the employee may contribute. Subscribers retain their PRAN when they leave, and choices of fund and allocation can be offered to employees."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Individuals who wish to act as retirement advisers to NPS subscribers are registered and regulated by:", ["SEBI", "IRDAI", "EPFO", "PFRDA"], 3, "Retirement advisers for NPS are registered with PFRDA under its retirement adviser regulations. SEBI regulates investment advisers for securities and IRDAI regulates insurance intermediaries."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "Interest on a Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme account is paid:", ["Only at maturity", "Monthly", "Half-yearly in arrears after the third year", "Quarterly"], 3, "SCSS pays interest every quarter, making it suitable for regular income. It is not cumulative and does not pay monthly."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Gratuity under the Payment of Gratuity Act is computed as 15/26 × last drawn salary × completed years of service. For a last drawn salary of ₹52,000 and 20 years of service, gratuity is:", ["₹6,00,000", "₹5,20,000", "₹10,40,000", "₹3,00,000"], 0, "Gratuity = 15 ÷ 26 × 52,000 × 20 = ₹6,00,000. ₹10,40,000 would ignore the 15/26 factor."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "Inflation averages 8% a year. Using the Rule of 72, roughly how long will it take for the purchasing power of a fixed pension to halve?", ["About 12 years", "About 6 years", "About 9 years", "About 18 years"], 2, "Under the Rule of 72, time to halve purchasing power ≈ 72 ÷ inflation rate = 72 ÷ 8 = 9 years. Twelve years would apply at 6% inflation."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "Partial withdrawals from NPS Tier I are calculated with reference to:", ["The subscriber's own contributions, not the employer's", "The entire corpus including employer contributions and returns", "Only the returns earned in the last financial year", "The annuity value expected at retirement"], 0, "Partial withdrawal limits are a percentage of the subscriber's own contributions (excluding employer contributions), subject to minimum holding period and number of withdrawals."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "In a defined-benefit pension plan, who mainly bears the investment risk?", ["The individual member", "The employer or plan sponsor", "The pension fund's custodian", "The member's nominee"], 1, "In a defined-benefit plan, the pension is fixed by formula, so the sponsor must make good any shortfall in fund returns. In a defined-contribution plan the member bears this risk."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "NPS Vatsalya accounts are opened for:", ["Senior citizens above 70 who cannot join regular NPS", "Minors, with contributions made by a parent or guardian", "Non-resident Indians only", "Government employees covered by the Unified Pension Scheme"], 1, "NPS Vatsalya allows parents or guardians to save for a minor's pension. It is operated by the guardian until the child reaches majority."], ["Retirement Planning: An Overview", "Why do many advisers recommend that retirement planning start in a person's twenties?", ["Tax laws allow retirement savings only for people below 30", "Longer compounding periods sharply reduce the monthly saving needed", "Pension products are not available to investors above 40", "Employers stop contributing to provident funds after 35"], 1, "The longer money compounds, the smaller the regular contribution needed to reach a target. There is no tax or product rule restricting retirement saving to young people."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "Why might a retiree prefer SCSS to an ordinary bank fixed deposit for part of his corpus?", ["It has no upper limit on investment", "It allows withdrawals at any time without any deduction or penalty on the interest", "It is government-backed and usually offers a higher rate than regular deposits", "Its interest is exempt from income tax"], 2, "SCSS carries sovereign backing and its rate is typically higher than standard bank deposit rates. It has an investment ceiling, premature closure attracts a deduction, and interest is taxable."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "Who among the following is eligible to open a Senior Citizens' Savings Scheme account before 60, subject to conditions?", ["Any salaried employee aged 45 or above", "Any NRI aged 55 or above", "A minor whose parent is a senior citizen", "A person who retired under a voluntary retirement scheme and meets the age and timing conditions"], 3, "SCSS is primarily for resident individuals aged 60 or more, but certain retirees, such as those on VRS or superannuation within the specified age band, may join earlier if they invest within the prescribed time of receiving benefits. NRIs cannot open SCSS."], ["Investment & Asset Allocation for Retirement", "A retiree compares two debt funds with similar risk. Fund A has an expense ratio of 0.3% and Fund B 1.2%. Over 20 years, the higher cost will most likely:", ["Have no effect because both funds hold similar bonds", "Increase returns, because higher fees usually mean better and more active management", "Matter only in the first year", "Reduce the final corpus noticeably due to compounding of the cost difference"], 3, "Costs compound over time, so an extra 0.9% a year significantly lowers final value. Higher fees do not guarantee better performance in similar-risk debt funds."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "Which person is NOT eligible to join Atal Pension Yojana under the current rules?", ["A 25-year-old domestic worker with a savings bank account", "An income-tax payer aged 30", "A 35-year-old farm labourer", "A 19-year-old street vendor"], 1, "Since October 2022, income-tax payers are not eligible to join APY. Other citizens within the entry age band can join through a bank or post office savings account."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "The monthly pension under the Employees' Pension Scheme is calculated as pensionable salary × pensionable service ÷ 70. For pensionable salary of ₹15,000 and 35 years of service, the pension is:", ["₹5,250", "₹15,000", "₹10,500", "₹7,500"], 3, "Pension = 15,000 × 35 ÷ 70 = ₹7,500 per month. ₹5,250 would result from dividing by 100 instead of 70."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "An NPS subscriber at exit has a very small accumulated corpus, below the threshold specified by PFRDA. Typically, she can:", ["Only buy an annuity with the entire corpus", "Not exit until the corpus crosses the threshold", "Withdraw the entire corpus as a lump sum without buying an annuity", "Transfer the corpus to her EPF account"], 2, "PFRDA allows full lump-sum withdrawal where the corpus is below a prescribed small-amount threshold, because an annuity on a tiny amount would be uneconomic."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "Which factor generally leads to a HIGHER annuity payout for the same purchase price?", ["Choosing the return-of-purchase-price option", "Adding a spouse under a joint-life option", "Choosing an annuity that increases every year", "Buying the annuity at an older age"], 3, "Older buyers have a shorter expected payout period, so insurers offer a higher rate. Return of purchase price, joint-life and increasing features all reduce the starting payout."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Which NPS-related deduction remains available to a salaried employee even under the new (default) tax regime?", ["Deduction for the employee's own additional NPS contribution of up to ₹50,000", "Deduction for contributions to NPS Tier II", "Deduction for contributions to Atal Pension Yojana", "Deduction for the employer's contribution to the employee's NPS account"], 3, "Under the new regime, most deductions are unavailable, but the employer's contribution to NPS remains deductible within the prescribed percentage of salary. The additional ₹50,000 deduction for own contribution is an old-regime benefit."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "A subscriber changes jobs from a private company to a different city. What happens to his NPS account?", ["He must close the account and open a new one with a fresh PRAN", "His corpus is transferred compulsorily to EPF", "The account is frozen until he reaches 60", "The same PRAN continues; only the employer or POP mapping changes"], 3, "NPS is portable: the PRAN stays the same across jobs and locations, and only the associated employer or POP changes. No closure or transfer to EPF is required."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Government sector NPS subscribers who do not exercise a choice have their contributions invested in:", ["Only the equity scheme of a single pension fund", "The Tier II account by default", "Atal Pension Yojana", "A default scheme split across pension funds in proportions set by the government and PFRDA"], 3, "For government employees, default contributions go to a default scheme managed by specified pension funds in prescribed proportions. Many government subscribers now also have the option to choose."], ["Pension Systems & Regulatory Framework (PFRDA)", "Which body administers the Employees' Provident Fund and the Employees' Pension Scheme for the organised private sector?", ["Pension Fund Regulatory and Development Authority", "National Pension System Trust", "Employees' Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO)", "Life Insurance Corporation of India"], 2, "EPF and EPS are administered by the EPFO under the EPF and Miscellaneous Provisions framework. PFRDA regulates NPS, not EPF."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "Why might a retiree combine an annuity with a mutual fund SWP rather than putting the entire corpus into an annuity?", ["Because annuities cannot be bought for more than ₹10 lakh", "Because SWPs guarantee a higher income than annuities for the rest of the retiree's life", "To get guaranteed lifelong income while keeping some growth and liquidity", "To avoid paying any tax on retirement income"], 2, "An annuity covers essential expenses for life, while an SWP portion offers growth potential and access to capital. Neither eliminates tax, and SWP income is not guaranteed."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A client's current monthly household expenses are ₹50,000. If inflation is 7% a year, what will these expenses be in 18 years (approximately)?", ["₹1.13 lakh", "₹1.28 lakh", "₹2.10 lakh", "₹1.69 lakh"], 3, "Future expense = 50,000 × 1.07^18 ≈ 50,000 × 3.38 ≈ ₹1.69 lakh a month. ₹1.13 lakh would result from simple rather than compound inflation."], ["Other Retirement Products (EPF, PPF, SCSS, APY, MFs)", "A retiree invests in a mutual fund and sets up a systematic withdrawal plan. Compared with a bank fixed deposit giving monthly interest, the SWP:", ["Guarantees a fixed return like a deposit", "Is fully exempt from tax in all cases, irrespective of holding period or the fund type", "Requires a lock-in of five years", "Can be more tax-efficient but its value fluctuates with the market"], 3, "SWP withdrawals are partly capital redemption, so only gains are taxed, making them potentially tax-efficient. However, the underlying units carry market risk, unlike a fixed deposit."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Ankit, 50, chooses Active Choice and wants to allocate the maximum permitted to equity. Which statement is correct?", ["There is no upper limit on equity allocation under Active Choice for any subscriber", "PFRDA caps equity under Active Choice and tapers the cap as the subscriber ages", "Active Choice permits equity only for subscribers under 35", "Equity is compulsory at 75% under Active Choice for everyone"], 1, "Active Choice lets subscribers decide allocation within limits; PFRDA sets a ceiling on equity which reduces with age in later years. There is no compulsory equity allocation."], ["Taxation of Retirement Benefits", "Employer contributions to an employee's EPF, NPS and superannuation fund together exceed the aggregate limit specified in tax law. The excess is:", ["Taxed as a perquisite in the employee's hands", "Exempt because retirement savings are never taxed", "Taxed in the employer's hands", "Refunded to the employer"], 0, "Tax law caps the aggregate employer contribution to these funds; the excess, along with related accretions, is taxable as a perquisite to the employee."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "A retiree buys an annuity with ₹60 lakh at a rate of 6.8% a year payable monthly. The approximate monthly annuity before tax is:", ["₹40,800", "₹34,000", "₹28,333", "₹3,40,000"], 1, "Annual annuity = 60 lakh × 6.8% = ₹4,08,000. Monthly ≈ 4,08,000 ÷ 12 = ₹34,000. ₹40,800 confuses the annual figure's decimal place."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "Partial withdrawal from NPS Tier I is allowed for specified purposes. Which is a permitted purpose?", ["Treatment of a specified critical illness of the subscriber or family", "Repayment of an outstanding credit card bill or personal loan of the subscriber", "Purchase of shares in an IPO", "Foreign holiday travel"], 0, "Partial withdrawals are allowed only for listed purposes such as children's education or marriage, house purchase or construction and treatment of specified illnesses. Consumption or investment needs do not qualify."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "If a subscriber in the all-citizen model does not indicate any investment choice, his contributions are invested under:", ["Active Choice with 100% government securities", "Auto Choice using the aggressive life-cycle fund", "Active Choice with an equal split across all asset classes", "Auto Choice using the moderate life-cycle fund"], 3, "Where no choice is made, NPS defaults to Auto Choice with the moderate life-cycle fund (LC50). Aggressive or conservative options must be selected actively by the subscriber."], ["NPS – Investment Options & Fund Management", "Which type of securities forms the main holding of the NPS 'C' asset class?", ["Corporate bonds and other fixed-income instruments of companies", "Commodities such as gold and silver", "Central government securities only, including treasury bills and state development loans", "Cash held with the trustee bank"], 0, "Asset class C is the corporate debt class, covering bonds and debt instruments of companies and similar entities. Government securities form asset class G."], ["NPS – Exit, Withdrawal & Annuity", "Compared with normal exit at the retirement age, premature exit from NPS before that age generally requires:", ["No annuity purchase at all", "The full corpus to be paid as lump sum", "A penalty equal to all employer contributions made since the account was opened", "A larger share of the corpus to be used for buying an annuity"], 3, "NPS discourages early exit by requiring a larger proportion to be annuitised on premature exit than on normal exit, except where the corpus is below the small-corpus threshold."], ["Retirement Planning Process & Corpus Estimation", "A plan was prepared five years ago assuming 6% inflation. Actual inflation has averaged 8%. The adviser should:", ["Keep the plan unchanged because it was prepared by a professional", "Review the assumptions and revise the corpus and saving targets", "Reduce the client's equity allocation to zero", "Advise the client to stop contributing until inflation falls"], 1, "Retirement plans need periodic review. Higher actual inflation increases future expenses, so assumptions and saving targets should be revised. Abandoning equity or stopping savings would worsen the gap."], ["National Pension System – Architecture", "Under the all-citizen model, an NPS subscriber's contribution passes through which sequence?", ["Pension fund → POP → trustee bank", "Custodian → CRA → POP", "POP → trustee bank → pension fund", "Annuity service provider → trustee bank → POP"], 2, "The subscriber contributes through a POP, which remits money to the trustee bank; after reconciliation with the CRA's records, funds go to the chosen pension fund for investment."], ["Distribution Phase & Annuities", "A deferred annuity is most suitable for a client who:", ["Needs income starting next month", "Wants to withdraw the entire amount in cash at any time", "Is still working and wants pension income to start some years later", "Has no need for any regular income"], 2, "A deferred annuity accumulates during a deferment period and starts payouts later, suiting people who are still working. Immediate annuities begin almost at once."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "An NPS subscriber's grievance has not been resolved satisfactorily by the intermediary within the specified time. A further escalation option available under PFRDA's framework is:", ["Filing a complaint with the EPFO", "Approaching the NPS Trust and then the ombudsman appointed under PFRDA regulations", "Approaching the Insurance Ombudsman for all NPS matters, including those involving POPs", "Escalating to the SEBI SCORES portal"], 1, "NPS grievances are lodged through CGMS; unresolved complaints can be escalated to the NPS Trust and then to the ombudsman under PFRDA regulations. EPFO and SEBI do not handle NPS grievances."], ["Ethics, Code of Conduct & Grievance Redressal", "An adviser receives a referral fee from an annuity provider whenever clients buy its annuities. She should:", ["Keep it confidential to avoid confusing clients", "Disclose the arrangement to clients before they decide", "Recommend only that provider's annuities to maximise income", "Refund the fee to the provider"], 1, "Material conflicts of interest, including referral fees, must be disclosed so clients can judge the advice. Disclosure does not remove the duty to recommend what is suitable."]]};
